{:edb/db-before-t 1625 :edb/db-after-t 1626 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534938 50 #inst "2026-10-08T11:33:40.225Z" 13194139534938 true] [17592186083229 1020 #edb/ref 17592186321169 13194139534938 true] [17592186087607 1020 #edb/ref 17592186321162 13194139534938 true] [17592186087930 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534938 true] [17592186087930 1100 "If $A = \\begin{bmatrix}k & k \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$ is a singular matrix, calculate the value of $k$." 13194139534938 false] [17592186087930 1100 "If $A=[\\begin{aligned}k & k \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]$ is a singular matrix, calculate the value of $k.$" 13194139534938 true] [17592186087930 1145 "The matrix $A$ is singular if and only if $det(A)=0.$ Calculating the determinant, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =k\\cdot 1-k\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =k-2k \\\\  & =-k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set the determinant equal to $0$ and solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-k & =0 \\\\ k & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of $k$ is $k=0.$" 13194139534938 true] [17592186096929 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534938 true] [17592186096929 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043368/assets/267d5f0aabe29da84842d2edd19276521640b838cfb1777e6932e7472716c04f.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the parabola shown above?" 13194139534938 true] [17592186096929 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1125/Source/Images/q-11823.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the parabola shown above?" 13194139534938 false] [17592186096929 1145 "From the diagram we see that the parabola is upward-opening, has its vertex at $(2,-4),$ and passes through the point $(6,8).$\n\nWe know that for any upward or downward-opening parabola with its vertex at $(h,k),$ the general standard equation is given by\n\n$$\n(x-h)^{2}=4p(y-k).\n$$\n\nHere, the vertex has coordinates $(h,k)=(2,-4).$ Substituting these into the general standard equation, we get\n\n$$\n(x-2)^{2}=4p(y+4).\n$$\n\nWe are left to find the value of $p.$ We know that the given parabola also passes through the point $(6,8),$ so we can substitute $x=6$ and $y=8$ into the standard equation and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2)^{2} & =4p(y+4) \\\\ {4}^{2} & =4p(8+4) \\\\ 16 & =48p \\\\ p & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2)^{2} & =4p(y+4) \\\\ (x-2)^{2} & =4(\\frac{1}{3})(y+4) \\\\ (x-2)^{2} & =\\frac{4}{3}(y+4).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534938 true] [17592186100904 1020 #edb/ref 17592186321146 13194139534938 true] [17592186105626 1020 #edb/ref 17592186321125 13194139534938 true] [17592186110496 1020 #edb/ref 17592186321155 13194139534938 true] [17592186129190 1020 #edb/ref 17592186321139 13194139534938 true] [17592186133861 1020 #edb/ref 17592186321132 13194139534938 true] [17592186321125 1017 #edb/ref 17592186321126 13194139534938 true] [17592186321125 1089 "q-101541" 13194139534938 true] [17592186321125 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534938 true] [17592186321125 1100 "Let $a=[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]$ and $b=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$ Find the vector projection of $a$ onto $b.$" 13194139534938 true] [17592186321125 1113 #uuid "2862ba25-b3dd-52fe-babb-88481e3608c2" 13194139534938 true] [17592186321125 1145 "Using the formula for the vector projection, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{b}\\,a & =\\frac{a\\cdot b}{b\\cdot b}b \\\\  & =\\frac{{a}_{x}{b}_{x}+{a}_{y}{b}_{y}+{a}_{z}{b}_{z}}{{b}_{x}^{2}+{b}_{y}^{2}+{b}_{z}^{2}}b \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\cdot 0+7\\cdot 2+(-4)\\cdot 3}{{0}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{3}^{2}}b \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{13}⟨0,2,3⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\frac{4}{13},\\frac{6}{13}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534938 true] [17592186321126 1000 #edb/ref 17592186321127 13194139534938 true] [17592186321126 1000 #edb/ref 17592186321128 13194139534938 true] [17592186321126 1000 #edb/ref 17592186321129 13194139534938 true] [17592186321126 1000 #edb/ref 17592186321130 13194139534938 true] [17592186321126 1000 #edb/ref 17592186321131 13194139534938 true] [17592186321126 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534938 true] [17592186321126 1041 "selection" 13194139534938 true] [17592186321126 1104 #edb/ref 17592186321128 13194139534938 true] [17592186321126 1110 #uuid "c67e4227-9229-5149-b649-60f2d95ff7ff" 13194139534938 true] [17592186321127 1034 "⟨0,0,0⟩" 13194139534938 true] [17592186321127 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534938 true] [17592186321127 1111 #uuid "1596b302-488d-52ec-b893-25831ccfd734" 13194139534938 true] [17592186321128 1034 "⟨0,\\frac{4}{13},\\frac{6}{13}⟩" 13194139534938 true] [17592186321128 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534938 true] [17592186321128 1111 #uuid "12f9e085-229a-5477-a113-3b693a34550f" 13194139534938 true] [17592186321129 1034 "⟨0,\\frac{2}{73},\\frac{3}{73}⟩" 13194139534938 true] [17592186321129 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534938 true] [17592186321129 1111 #uuid "17699248-2fe7-5e8b-932f-b2cbf1cfc70a" 13194139534938 true] [17592186321130 1034 "⟨0,\\frac{4}{73},\\frac{6}{73}⟩" 13194139534938 true] [17592186321130 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534938 true] [17592186321130 1111 #uuid "60b69ecc-1940-5d08-8109-42cd6b7098e8" 13194139534938 true] [17592186321131 1034 "⟨0,\\frac{2}{13},\\frac{3}{13}⟩" 13194139534938 true] [17592186321131 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534938 true] [17592186321131 1111 #uuid "4f0c760e-2668-5284-a218-14ba16d6f385" 13194139534938 true] [17592186321132 1017 #edb/ref 17592186321133 13194139534938 true] [17592186321132 1089 "q-116604" 13194139534938 true] [17592186321132 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534938 true] [17592186321132 1100 "Solve the inequality ${x}^{3}-9x<0.$" 13194139534938 true] [17592186321132 1113 #uuid "e9c567d1-1fe1-5af5-9c79-4a83502f3a73" 13194139534938 true] [17592186321132 1145 "First, we factor the polynomial on the left-hand side of the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}-9x & =x({x}^{2}-9) \\\\  & =x(x+3)(x-3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we define the function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf(x)=x(x+3)(x-3).\n$$\n\nSo we're now solving the inequality $f(x)<0.$\n\nThe solutions to the equation $f(x)=0$ are $x=-3,$ $x=0$ and $x=3.$ These roots divide the number line into $4$ subintervals:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,-3),\\,(-3,0),\\,(0,3),\\,(3,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-3)$ | $(-3,0)$ | $(0,3)$ | $(3,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x+3$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x-3$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the inequality $f(x)<0,$ we look at the bottom row to determine where $f(x)$ is negative.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(x)$ is negative on the intervals $(-\\infty ,-3)$ and $(0,3).$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $f(x)<0$ for all $x\\in (-\\infty ,-3)\\cup (0,3).$" 13194139534938 true] [17592186321133 1000 #edb/ref 17592186321134 13194139534938 true] [17592186321133 1000 #edb/ref 17592186321135 13194139534938 true] [17592186321133 1000 #edb/ref 17592186321136 13194139534938 true] [17592186321133 1000 #edb/ref 17592186321137 13194139534938 true] [17592186321133 1000 #edb/ref 17592186321138 13194139534938 true] [17592186321133 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534938 true] [17592186321133 1041 "selection" 13194139534938 true] [17592186321133 1104 #edb/ref 17592186321134 13194139534938 true] [17592186321133 1110 #uuid "9fcc8a73-a7fe-596a-930a-66c45e5d8b45" 13194139534938 true] [17592186321134 1034 "x\\in (-\\infty ,-3)\\cup (0,3)" 13194139534938 true] [17592186321134 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534938 true] [17592186321134 1111 #uuid "af810eeb-2e02-5de1-9a92-78e849928924" 13194139534938 true] [17592186321135 1034 "x\\in (-\\infty ,-3)\\cup (3,\\infty )" 13194139534938 true] [17592186321135 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534938 true] [17592186321135 1111 #uuid "9852b6af-fd76-5843-a953-8840419120f6" 13194139534938 true] [17592186321136 1034 "x\\in (0,3)" 13194139534938 true] [17592186321136 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534938 true] [17592186321136 1111 #uuid "f5f892ab-2f7f-59da-84a9-e11e66f71bd2" 13194139534938 true] [17592186321137 1034 "x\\in (3,\\infty )" 13194139534938 true] [17592186321137 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534938 true] [17592186321137 1111 #uuid "2a59e46b-f97a-5d54-a12f-48e06ab3c12d" 13194139534938 true] [17592186321138 1034 "x\\in (-\\infty ,-3)" 13194139534938 true] [17592186321138 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534938 true] [17592186321138 1111 #uuid "b1c23517-1d73-5b59-bccd-79e259cffab0" 13194139534938 true] [17592186321139 1017 #edb/ref 17592186321140 13194139534938 true] [17592186321139 1089 "q-143077" 13194139534938 true] [17592186321139 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534938 true] [17592186321139 1100 "In a controlled environment, the temperature $T$ of a food container decreases at a rate that's inversely proportional to the square of the temperature. What differential equation describes the rate of change of the temperature of the container $T?$" 13194139534938 true] [17592186321139 1113 #uuid "b4bd1c51-7cdb-5faf-a2e7-d35b9e8b6eb2" 13194139534938 true] [17592186321139 1145 "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*In a controlled environment, the **temperature** $T$ of a food container **decreases** at a rate that's **inversely proportional** to the **square of the temperature.** *\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *temperature $T$* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}$ is *inversely proportional to* the *square of the temperature $T.$* In other words,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}∝\\frac{1}{{T}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nRecall that $∝$ means \"is proportional to.\"\n\n- Finally, we want to replace the proportionality symbol with an equality symbol. Let $k$ be a positive constant. Since the temperature *decreases* with time, our derivative should be *negative*. Therefore, we can rewrite our proportionality statement as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{k}{{T}^{2}}.\n$$" 13194139534938 true] [17592186321140 1000 #edb/ref 17592186321141 13194139534938 true] [17592186321140 1000 #edb/ref 17592186321142 13194139534938 true] [17592186321140 1000 #edb/ref 17592186321143 13194139534938 true] [17592186321140 1000 #edb/ref 17592186321144 13194139534938 true] [17592186321140 1000 #edb/ref 17592186321145 13194139534938 true] [17592186321140 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534938 true] [17592186321140 1041 "selection" 13194139534938 true] [17592186321140 1104 #edb/ref 17592186321143 13194139534938 true] [17592186321140 1110 #uuid "dca9088a-3574-5fb0-a2c2-bca04f47d657" 13194139534938 true] [17592186321141 1034 "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=k{T}^{2},\\,k>0" 13194139534938 true] [17592186321141 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534938 true] [17592186321141 1111 #uuid "8041938c-b394-5c87-9c43-eb0775333a0a" 13194139534938 true] [17592186321142 1034 "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k{T}^{2},\\,k>0" 13194139534938 true] [17592186321142 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534938 true] [17592186321142 1111 #uuid "b2975091-274c-575a-9ca4-99f242ca3d74" 13194139534938 true] [17592186321143 1034 "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{k}{{T}^{2}},\\,k>0" 13194139534938 true] [17592186321143 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534938 true] [17592186321143 1111 #uuid "e23dcaae-343e-5c6f-ab58-e9e96baff3e4" 13194139534938 true] [17592186321144 1034 "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-kT,\\,k>0" 13194139534938 true] [17592186321144 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534938 true] [17592186321144 1111 #uuid "bc2818cb-bca7-5dbf-ab48-fe00791e051c" 13194139534938 true] [17592186321145 1034 "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{k}{{T}^{2}},\\,k>0" 13194139534938 true] [17592186321145 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534938 true] [17592186321145 1111 #uuid "28774533-52cb-5376-a3bd-b7e727f3538f" 13194139534938 true] [17592186321146 1017 #edb/ref 17592186321147 13194139534938 true] [17592186321146 1017 #edb/ref 17592186321149 13194139534938 true] [17592186321146 1017 #edb/ref 17592186321151 13194139534938 true] [17592186321146 1017 #edb/ref 17592186321153 13194139534938 true] [17592186321146 1089 "q-279528" 13194139534938 true] [17592186321146 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534938 true] [17592186321146 1100 "Calculate $X+(Y-Z)$, where\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 6 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{aligned}],\\,Y=[\\begin{aligned}9 & 8 \\\\ 3 & 7\\end{aligned}],\\,Z=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n| $X+(Y-Z)=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} |  |  |" 13194139534938 true] [17592186321146 1113 #uuid "3bd04c9e-65fe-587f-81f5-e3a3e40c5df8" 13194139534938 true] [17592186321146 1145 "Performing the operations on each corresponding entry, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X+(Y-Z) & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 6 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{aligned}]+([\\begin{aligned}9 & 8 \\\\ 3 & 7\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 6 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}9-2 & 8-3 \\\\ 3-1 & 7-4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 6 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}7 & 5 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4+7 & 6+5 \\\\ 2+2 & 5+3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}11 & 11 \\\\ 4 & 8\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534938 true] [17592186321147 1000 #edb/ref 17592186321148 13194139534938 true] [17592186321147 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534938 true] [17592186321147 1041 "field-1" 13194139534938 true] [17592186321147 1104 #edb/ref 17592186321148 13194139534938 true] [17592186321147 1110